# Vercel runs scripts/vercel-ignore.sh on Linux. A CRLF checkout there makes
# bash fail on the shebang line, and the failure shows up as a broken deploy
# rather than as anything about line endings. Pin it, whatever core.autocrlf
# is set to locally.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Same reason, for everything that only ever runs on the Linux server. A CR at
# the end of a systemd directive or an nginx directive is a parse error there.
*.service text eol=lf
*.conf    text eol=lf
backend/ops/linux/** text eol=lf

# Windows tooling reads these; keep them CRLF so Notepad and sc.exe behave.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Not the .cnf files. They are read by MySQL, on Linux as well as Windows, and
# a trailing CR can end up inside a config value rather than being stripped.
*.cnf text eol=lf

# Piped into mysql on the server. The deploy script strips CR defensively, but
# there is no reason to put it there in the first place.
*.sql text eol=lf

# Brand files are byte copies of the kit (README.md has the map), so Git must
# never rewrite their line endings. With core.autocrlf=true a Windows checkout
# turns LF into CRLF in anything it detects as text, and it detects the kit's
# PDFs as text: every byte offset in the xref table then points at the wrong
# place and the PDF no longer opens. The SVGs survive that, but stop matching
# the kit's sha256, which is how a copy is checked.
Logo/**           -text
public/brand/**   -text
src/app/icon.svg  -text
*.pdf             binary
